\section{Precision}{

Each block is computed to an \strong{absolute} precision of about machine
epsilon times the size of the model's coefficients, not relative to the
block itself. Re-projecting the stored terms adds the dominance and pair
coefficients into the additive one (\verb{alpha = a + b d + sum e c}), so an
additive block many orders of magnitude below the dominance or A x A
block keeps few correct digits: a model stored with an additive variance
of exactly \code{1e-24} beside a dominance variance of 1 is reported as about
\code{1.0001e-24}. At ordinary ratios the error is at rounding level.
